side_effects: "A Gunslinger builds a reputation and its enemies. Some communities welcome those known as lawmen or champions of the downtrodden; most fear, distrust and hate paid killers. He is obvious from the pair of handguns, the clothes or ornate armour, and the way he carries himself, and since the reputation is itself a weapon he rarely tries to hide what he is. Anyone travelling in his company may be judged equally undesirable."
extraction_notes: "The book''s own survival figures, kept as lore rather than as mechanics: 97.5% of professional Gunslingers die by gunfire, a third of those in an ambush and a third in a showdown; 2.5% retire or die naturally. Fewer than 100 famous Gunslingers of 7th-15th level exist, with perhaps 1,200 more veterans across North America. Standard Equipment stores only the catalog rows that exist; the book also lists M.D.C. body armour of choice, tinted goggles, a quality cowboy hat, riding clothes, an expensive town suit, a multi-optics band, a laser distancer, a pocket translator, a gun cleaning kit, a cigarette lighter, a pocket mirror, a silver cross, 2 pairs of handcuffs, 50 ft of rope, a two-holster gun belt, a laser scalpel, a Derringer, a pair of silver-plated revolvers with 60-96 silver bullets, a pair of automatic pistols, a pair of energy pistols, one more weapon of choice including a TW revolver, 1D4+4 spare E-clips each and 288 rounds per weapon, and a robot horse or a new hovercycle or land rover of choice."
