# Gunslinger

**Alignments.** Any, but often anarchist or evil. Those who are honourable and
live by the Code of the New West may be any alignment, including good,
unprincipled or aberrant.

A popular saying in the New West holds that the only thing meaner, deadlier and
lower than a rattlesnake is a Gunslinger, which is a fair summary of how most
people regard the trade. They are feared and widely taken for paid assassins
and murdering brigands, and the worst of them have earned it - killers who
enjoy the work, or who like being respected out of fear, or who have simply
gone cold to other people''s suffering.

Not all of them are evil. Some are lawmen, local champions, manhunters,
vigilantes or renowned adventurers who deal out hard justice through fast
thinking and faster guns. Many of the ones who end up vile started as brash
teenagers who thought themselves quicker and smarter than anyone else, or who
set out to avenge somebody.

## The odds

Three-quarters of those overconfident young guns are killed or crippled in
their first gunfight, before the career begins at all. A showdown against a
seasoned Gunslinger of 5th level or higher almost always ends with the young
one dead. Another fifth die within their first year, rarely passing 3rd level.
Roughly one in twenty survives long enough to build anything worth calling a
career, and half of those die violently within seven years.

## The work

An expert in handguns, sharpshooting and the fast draw, the Gunslinger is a
mercenary who specialises in protection, enforcement and murder. Criminal
kingpins, wealthy ranchers, businessmen and bandit leaders hire them as hit men
and enforcers.

Two weapons matter: the revolvers and the reputation. The reputation, real or
exaggerated, frightens off opponents and ends conflicts - sometimes a warning
or simply being present will stop a brawl - and it is what he uses to enforce
his employer''s will. He is not shy about the guns either.

## Lore

Also known as the ''Slinger - common slang in the New West, especially for those
of good or unprincipled alignment - and as the Shootist, the Duelist, the Hired
Gun, the Killer and the Assassin.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'gunslinger');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'gunslinger';

